 succeeded in 0ms:
58:        <p class="hero__promise"><strong>Mastery promise.</strong> By the end, you will be able to diagnose agentic work with the Core Four, explain the exact difference between AI coding and agentic coding, reproduce Dan’s interactive and headless demos, and teach why “replace yourself” is a leverage strategy rather than a surrender.</p>
107:          <p>You type zero lines of application code by hand. “Stop coding” is a behavioral constraint, not a discussion topic. You may type prompts and terminal commands. When a generated file is imperfect, repair it through the agent so you practice controlling the loop.</p>
114:        <p class="section-lead">The title matters only if the work changes. “Agentic Engineer” is not a badge for someone who uses AI often; it names an engineer who builds systems able to take engineering actions.</p>
120:          <p>This is why “prompt engineer” is too narrow for Dan’s prediction. A clever request can improve a model’s answer. An agentic system changes who performs the sequence. If the human must relay every output into the next input, the human is still the orchestration layer. The system has intelligence but little agency.</p>
154:          <p>Dan opens with a concrete personal bet: in 2023 he bought <em>agenticengineer.com</em>, predicting a role defined by “the ability to build systems that can take actions as we can.” The claim is not that engineers vanish. It is that an engineer’s leverage shifts from personally performing actions to constructing systems that can perform them.</p>
159:          <p>His one move is conceptual before it is technical: name the era change. In phase one, prompt-to-code makes code abundant and gives rise to vibe coding. In phase two, the engineering response is to command systems that take actions. That frame prevents the later terminal demo from shrinking into “Claude Code can run Git.” Git commands are merely the visible actions; role redesign is the lesson.</p>
203:          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you say “agentic” because the tool has an agent mode, but you still approve and relay each micro-step. Or you send a long prompt that generates several files but never executes or verifies them.</p>
230:            <p><strong>Explain in 60 seconds:</strong> “An Agentic Engineer is not just someone who writes better prompts.” Use one example from the seven-file TAC-1 repository and name the moment when the human stops being workflow glue.</p>
231:            <p class="fine">Pass if your explanation includes actions, a repeatable system, and a report—not merely “AI does more.”</p>
276:          <p>Dan states the mission with uncomfortable specificity: become an engineer “they” cannot replace—where “they” means your boss, the company, the industry, and AI. The point is not permanent safety; he explicitly says nothing in technology lasts forever. The point is to choose the adaptation with the greatest asymmetric advantage.</p>
287:          <p>The story’s one move is to replace a threatened identity with a higher-order one. If “real engineer” means “person who types the best code,” an agent that types better feels like a rival. If engineering means building systems of leverage that produce valuable outcomes, a capable coding agent becomes new machinery. You are measured by what you can make the machinery reliably accomplish.</p>
351:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Why does Dan say the “10x” number is arbitrary?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Because the multiplier comes from the leverage point you find. It could be 2x, 10x, or 100x; it does not come from a fixed amount of greater effort.</p></details></div>
374:          <p>An agent produces a nearly correct file. One import is wrong and one edge case is missing. You think, “It will be faster if I just fix those two lines.” You open the editor, type the repair, and move on. The task is complete, but the learning loop is broken.</p>
376:          <p>This is the behavioral edge of “stop coding.” It does not ask whether you are capable of the fix. It asks whether your personal typing is still the best lever for an outcome you expect to repeat.</p>
408:            <figcaption>“Stop coding” removes the human from the typing center, not from the engineering system.</figcaption>
415:          <p>At 6:47, after establishing the mission and leverage frame, Dan reveals the first tactic: “Stop coding.” He repeats it. Then he sharpens the claim: your hands and your mind are no longer the best tool for the job of writing code. The directness is meant to break a default behavior, not to erase engineering knowledge.</p>
470:            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>“No more code”</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p><strong>Why it fails:</strong> code remains a primary asset that produces features and products. TAC expects you to generate more of it.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> say “stop hand-typing” every time you teach the tactic.</p></div></article>
471:            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>“No need to understand code”</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p><strong>Why it fails:</strong> faster generation magnifies the cost of weak review and weak system judgment.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> move reading, execution, tests, and diff review into explicit pass criteria.</p></div></article>
507:            <p><strong>Teach the tactic to a junior developer</strong> without using the phrase “AI is better at coding.” Explain what they stop, what they keep, and what three kinds of work replace typing. Give one example of a good re-prompt after an imperfect file.</p>
521:          <p>An agentic run stops before committing. A weak operator submits the same prompt again, perhaps adding “please make sure to commit.” The second run stops too. The operator concludes that the model is flaky.</p>
523:          <p>Without a diagnostic vocabulary, every failure looks like “bad AI.” With the Core Four, you can isolate the broken dimension before spending another token.</p>
542:            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Context</b><p>“Can it see what matters?”</p></div>
543:            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Model</b><p>“Can it reason through this?”</p></div>
544:            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Prompt</b><p>“Does it know the outcome and checks?”</p></div>
545:            <div class="concept-strip__item concept-strip__item--new"><b>Tools</b><p>“Can it take the required actions?”</p></div>
557:          <p>Dan anticipates the obvious objection: tool calling is not new. His answer is exact and portable: “It’s not a question of new. It’s a question of scale and performance.” A single calculator call proves a model can use a tool. A branch → write → run → observe → stage → commit → report chain proves that tool use can form a unit of engineering work.</p>
622:          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you can chant “context, model, prompt, tools” but respond to every error by rewriting the prompt.</p>
642:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What is Dan’s rebuttal to “tool calling isn’t new”?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It is not a question of new; it is a question of scale and performance.</p></details></div>
651:            <p class="fine">Pass if you use evidence rather than “the AI is flaky.”</p>
663:          <h3>“Both made files, so they are basically the same”</h3>
802:          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> your A/B notes say “Prompt A made one Python file; Prompt B made another Python file.”</p>
845:          <p>“Which model is best?” is therefore too small a question for agentic coding. The unit under evaluation is the combined system. TAC-1’s agentic prompt requires the system to interpret a runbook, plan it, call file and shell tools in order, preserve outputs as context, and know when the requested end state has been reached. A high benchmark score on code generation does not prove that chain.</p>
877:          <h3>From “why now?” to an evaluation frame</h3>
908:              <tr><td>Interprets “new/existing branch” and a behavioral file description</td><td>Reasoning model</td><td>The prompt is not a fixed command script; meaning must be converted into actions.</td></tr>
917:            <p>Dan states that TAC will use millions of tokens across the full course and calls the investment “pay to play.” The chapter does not invent a dollar estimate; costs and plans change. The operational lesson is to budget enough model access that you can run the exercises rather than stopping at recognition.</p>
937:          <p><strong>Worship:</strong> “Claude Code is the lesson.” This makes every UI change feel like curriculum drift and hides the transferable checklist.</p>
938:          <p><strong>Dismissal:</strong> “Any chat model can call a tool, so there is no lesson.” This ignores long-chain reliability, state management, and action architecture.</p>
1057:          <p>Dan’s key expansion is spatial: “It’s not just about the agentic prompt. It’s about <strong>where</strong> we can use the agentic prompt.” A prompt trapped in an interactive session depends on your presence. A prompt callable from code can live inside triggers and environments where you are absent. TAC-1 names triggers as later-course material; this lesson only proves the callable primitive.</p>
1114:          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you read the three lines of Shell, say “I get it,” and skip the run. The concept stays intellectually obvious but behaviorally uninstalled.</p>
1116:          <p>Other common stalls are state errors. If you run from <code>demo-agentic-coding</code> rather than <code>main</code>, the starting state no longer matches the demonstration. If the branch already exists, “new/existing” may legitimately reuse it. Reset between runs so launcher equivalence is what you test.</p>
1144:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Why is the “agent as function” analogy imperfect?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The call has side effects in the repository and terminal. Starting state, permissions, reports, and exit status therefore matter.</p></details></div>
1151:            <p><strong>Explain the Python launcher line by line</strong> to someone who thinks “programmable agent” requires a special platform. Then explain why the REPORT and return code matter once the human is not present.</p>
1166:          <p>The engineer can protect that dependency, repeating the ritual forever, or encode it into a bounded, reviewable system. Encoding seems to remove their immediate necessity. It also changes their contribution from “person who remembers the steps” to “person who understands, improves, and governs the release system.” The first kind of indispensability is a bottleneck. The second is leverage.</p>
1187:          <p>The replacement is narrow and exact: Dan is no longer necessary to paste the prompt and supervise each transition of this branch-write-run-commit-report workflow. He remains necessary to choose the workflow, explain why it matters, define the permissions, inspect the report, and decide what mechanism to build next. This narrowness protects the idea from hype. You do not replace “the engineer” in one move. You replace one repeated piece of the engineer’s current execution.</p>
1201:          <p>Plain English: systematically turn your repeated execution into systems, then move your attention to the judgment and design those systems need. “Replace yourself” is a method for discovering the next level of your role. It does not guarantee permanent job safety; Dan explicitly frames evolution as continuous. It creates an asymmetric advantage because you learn to own the machinery of change rather than defend the task being changed.</p>
1229:            <li>Complete the sentence: “I am not making myself unnecessary; I am making my attention available for ___.”</li>
1237:          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> “If I automate my work, the company will not need me, so I should protect the manual process.” This treats brittle task ownership as career security.</p>
1258:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Why is “this launcher replaces an engineer” a bad interpretation?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It overstates a narrow workflow replacement. Credible progress names the exact repeated execution removed and the higher-order work that remains.</p></details></div>
1318:            <strong>Checkpoint 0 · no notes.</strong> Say: “Read, Write, Edit; UV run; Git checkout, branch, add, commit; WebSearch; no push permission.” If you cannot retrieve the surface, reread the file. Permission literacy is a graded outcome.
1329:          <p><strong>Failure this exposes:</strong> mistaking “more generated files” for “engineering work carried by an agent.”</p>
1416:            <strong>Checkpoint 2 · one sentence.</strong> “The host language does not matter because any program with terminal access can pass the shared prompt to <code>claude -p</code>; the prompt and agent runtime define the engineering workflow.”
1455:            <li>Record the exact transition where the system halts, refuses, or asks. Do not summarize it as “Claude failed.”</li>
1457:            <li>Complete the sentence: “The prompt described the commit, but the workflow could not cross that state boundary because ___.”</li>
1535:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What does “Stop Coding” forbid, and what does it preserve?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It forbids hand-typing application code as the default instrument. It preserves and intensifies code generation, understanding, review, planning, and closed-loop design.</p></details></div>
1536:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Define Commander of Compute without saying “uses AI.”</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>An engineer who aims compute by specifying outcomes, context, constraints, bounded actions, evidence, and triggers to build systems that build systems.</p></details></div>
1560:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">16. Resolve “replace yourself” in one sentence.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Automate your repeated execution so your scarce attention moves to judgment, system ownership, and the next leverage point.</p></details></div>
1588:          <div><dt>Loot box</dt><dd>The per-lesson collection of prompts, links, notes, and assets. The transcript may mishear it as “loop box.”</dd></div>
1606:          <div><dt>Leverage</dt><dd>More valuable outcome per unit of scarce human attention. The “10x” label is arbitrary; the leverage point determines the multiplier.</dd></div>
1608:          <div><dt>Contradicting truth</dt><dd>“To become an irreplaceable engineer, you will replace yourself”: encode repeated execution and move to higher-order judgment and ownership.</dd></div>

