+      <section class="chapter-section" id="mastery">
+        <span class="section-number">10</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Mastery check · retrieve, diagnose, teach</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Recognition is not the finish line. Close the chapter, answer on paper, then return to reveal. Score operations and reasoning, not exact prose.</p>
+
+        <h3>Round 1 · Foundations</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative foundations quiz">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What are the Big Three?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Context, model, prompt—present in AI coding whether or not they are visible in the interface.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What upgrades them to the Core Four?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Tools, specifically prompts that can reliably execute long chains of tool calls.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What does “Stop Coding” forbid, and what does it preserve?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It forbids hand-typing application code as the default instrument. It preserves and intensifies code generation, understanding, review, planning, and closed-loop design.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Define Commander of Compute without saying “uses AI.”</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>An engineer who aims compute by specifying outcomes, context, constraints, bounded actions, evidence, and triggers to build systems that build systems.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round 2 · Trace the demo</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative demo quiz">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">5. Reconstruct Prompt B’s state transitions without looking.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Checkout the demo branch; create <code>main_tac.py</code>; run <code>main_aic.py</code>; run <code>main_tac.py</code>; stage files; commit with the specified message; report completion.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">6. Which runtime artifact must exist before the README Agentic Coding Prompt runs, and why?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>main_aic.py</code>, because Prompt B runs it but does not create it. The interactive A/B order supplies it.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">7. What exact commit subject proves the Git portion completed?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>Demo agentic coding capabilities</code>.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">8. What makes <code>prompt.md</code> self-contained and observable?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It creates both Python files, performs the branch/run/commit chain, and demands exact program outputs in REPORT.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round 3 · Diagnose with the Core Four</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative diagnosis quiz">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">9. The prompt says commit; the model plans commit; the agent cannot execute it after S3. Which dimension failed?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Tools/permission: the commit tool pattern was removed from the allowlist.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">10. The agent edits the wrong <code>prompt.md</code> in a larger repo. What two dimensions should you probe first?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Context—what paths and repository content it saw—and prompt—whether the target path was explicit.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">11. A tool calls all commands but forgets the first program’s output before REPORT. Which essential element is most suspect?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The agent architecture’s state/observation management, though the combined system should be evaluated rather than blaming a label alone.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">12. Why is repeated rerolling a weak diagnostic method?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It changes stochastic output without isolating whether context, model, prompt, or tools caused the failed state transition.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round 4 · Transfer and judgment</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative transfer quiz">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">13. A prompt generates five excellent files, but a human runs tests, commits, and reports. AI coding or agentic coding?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Primarily AI coding. Output quantity does not move the workflow boundary; the human still carries the engineering transitions.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">14. Why is exact report-back a seed of closed-loop engineering?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It turns internal work into an externally checkable claim that a human or another program can compare with reality and use for the next decision.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">15. When is a tiny workflow a better leverage point than a huge one?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>When it repeats often, has clear state transitions and evidence, and can be carried reliably with low marginal human attention.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">16. Resolve “replace yourself” in one sentence.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Automate your repeated execution so your scarce attention moves to judgment, system ownership, and the next leverage point.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <aside class="callout callout--why">
+          <p class="callout__label">Scoring</p>
+          <p><strong>13–16 correct:</strong> ready to teach and proceed. <strong>9–12:</strong> rerun the lab checkpoint associated with each miss. <strong>0–8:</strong> redraw the phase map and Core Four, then reproduce the A/B test again. A recognized answer that you cannot use in diagnosis counts as half credit.</p>
+        </aside>
+
+        <h3>Capstone teach-back</h3>
+        <div class="teach-back">
+          <p>Without notes, teach a colleague for five minutes:</p>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Why reliable long tool chains—not tool calling itself—separate agentic coding from AI coding.</li>
+            <li>How the two README prompts provide evidence.</li>
+            <li>What the Core Four are and how to use them to diagnose a failed commit.</li>
+            <li>What <code>claude -p</code> unlocks that an interactive session does not.</li>
+            <li>Why replacing the operator of one workflow can make the engineer more valuable.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <p class="fine">Expert pass: your listener can reconstruct the course workflow and predict the effect of removing <code>Bash(git commit:*)</code>.</p>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="glossary">
+        <span class="section-number">11</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">TAC-1 glossary</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Use Dan’s terms consistently. Each definition below is narrow enough to test against the lesson repository.</p>
+        <dl class="glossary">
+          <div><dt>TAC</dt><dd>Tactical Agentic Coding: eight lessons, eight tactics, and one course codebase per lesson.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Loot box</dt><dd>The per-lesson collection of prompts, links, notes, and assets. The transcript may mishear it as “loop box.”</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>AI coding</dt><dd>Phase-one prompt-to-code work governed by context, model, and prompt. Useful and foundational, but the human often carries the surrounding workflow.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Agentic coding</dt><dd>Phase-two engineering in which the Big Three gain tools and a prompt reliably carries a chain of actions.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Agentic Engineer</dt><dd>An engineer who builds systems that can take engineering actions, rather than only prompting for isolated artifacts.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Commander of compute</dt><dd>The engineer who aims model capacity through outcomes, context, constraints, tools, evidence, and eventually triggers.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Big Three</dt><dd>Context, model, prompt—the primitives present in every AI coding interaction.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Core Four</dt><dd>Context, model, prompt, tools—the phase-two diagnostic vocabulary.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Context</dt><dd>Everything the model can see while working: repository files, current state, settings, instructions, and tool observations.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Model</dt><dd>The language model performing interpretation, reasoning, generation, and decisions inside the agentic system.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Prompt</dt><dd>The instruction describing outcome, starting state, constraints, actions, checks, and report. In TAC-1 it becomes a runbook.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Tools</dt><dd>The bounded actions an agent may perform. Phase-two significance comes from reliable long chains, not mere tool existence.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Agentic Coding Prompt</dt><dd>A workflow specification containing a sequence of tool calls. TAC-1 uses RUN, CREATE, RUN, and later REPORT.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Long tool chain</dt><dd>Several ordered actions whose observations and state must carry forward reliably toward one outcome.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Programmable agentic coding</dt><dd>Calling an agentic tool from code. In TAC-1, Shell, Python, and TypeScript all pass <code>prompt.md</code> to <code>claude -p</code>.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>REPORT</dt><dd>The prompt block requiring returned evidence. TAC-1 asks for exact outputs of both generated Python files.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Closed loop</dt><dd>A structure where action produces observations that can be checked and used for the next decision rather than ending at generation.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>System that builds systems</dt><dd>Dan’s description of moving up the stack: construct mechanisms that create and operate software-producing mechanisms.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Self-operating machine</dt><dd>The incremental end-state of a codebase whose agentic workflows increasingly run at the right time with bounded oversight.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Leverage</dt><dd>More valuable outcome per unit of scarce human attention. The “10x” label is arbitrary; the leverage point determines the multiplier.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Stop Coding</dt><dd>Tactic #1: stop hand-typing application code while increasing planning, review, code understanding, and closed-loop design.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Contradicting truth</dt><dd>The paradox that automating your repeated execution moves you toward higher-order judgment, improvement, and system ownership.</dd></div>
+        </dl>
+      </section>
+
+      <footer class="chapter-footer">
+        <p class="eyebrow">Volume I · Tactical Agentic Coding</p>
